commonMistakes:
  - title: "Using Math.random() for UUIDs"
    description: "Standard JavaScript Math.random() is pseudorandom and predictable. Always require crypto.randomUUID() or crypto.getRandomValues() to prevent identifier collision and forgery."
  - title: "Treating UUIDs as Secret Passwords"
    description: "UUIDs are unique identifiers, not authentication tokens or cryptographic keys. They lack cryptographic hashing or salt and should not substitute for secrets."
  - title: "Assuming Sequential Monotonicity"
    description: "Version 4 UUIDs are completely random and non-sequential. Using them as clustered database primary keys without sorting indexes can cause B-tree fragmentation."
  - title: "Case-Sensitive Database Lookups"
    description: "RFC 4122 specifies that hexadecimal comparisons must be case-insensitive. Failing to normalize case when querying databases can lead to missing records."
