faqs:
  - q: "What is a Version 4 UUID?"
    a: "A Version 4 UUID is a 128-bit universally unique identifier generated using random numbers. It contains 122 random bits, giving 5.3 × 10³⁶ possible values with virtually zero probability of duplicate generation."
  - q: "What is the difference between a UUID and a GUID?"
    a: "GUID (Globally Unique Identifier) is Microsoft's implementation term for UUID (Universally Unique Identifier). In modern software, both refer to identical 128-bit RFC 4122 compliant structures."
  - q: "How are UUIDs generated securely in this tool?"
    a: "This tool uses the native browser Web Crypto API (crypto.randomUUID). Entropy is gathered directly from your operating system's cryptographic random hardware generator."
  - q: "Can two UUID v4 values collide?"
    a: "While theoretically possible, generating 1 billion UUIDs per second for 100 years yields less than a 50% chance of a single collision. For practical software engineering, UUIDs are universally unique."
  - q: "Are generated UUIDs transmitted or logged?"
    a: "Never. Generation executes 100% client-side inside your browser memory. No generated keys or logs ever touch external servers."
